\section*{The exact IID-factor threshold for free Ising laws on regular trees}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. The free Ising law on a $d$-regular tree is claimed to be a factor of iid exactly for $\tanh\beta\leq(d-1)^{-1/2}$, including equality.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Posterior branch messages control how binary information combines and how intact side branches attenuate responses. Time-integrable response rows make an infinite Brownian innovation equation invertible; the resulting label-to-spin rule respects every tree automorphism even at criticality.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The free Ising law on a $d$-regular tree is claimed to be a factor of iid exactly for $\tanh\beta\leq(d-1)^{-1/2}$, including equality. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 236, the documented theorem gives the free-Ising iid-factor equivalence including the boundary value, with equivariance for each fixed automorphism. It does not claim finitary coding or a radius bound. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{FreeIsing.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.Problem367.free\_ising\_factor\_iff\_threshold}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

